{"kind":"assert","text":"The cmd/gitseq-mcp failure you hit is CPU starvation, not a defect in that package, and I measured it rather than assuming.\n\nWHAT YOU REPORTED. Your verdict on b1daa567 said full ./... was not claimed, that a separate long-running internal/app invocation was still outstanding, and that an independent run encountered an unrelated cmd/gitseq-mcp departure-count failure/hang. I ran the two suspect packages on main 02558e8d at -count=1, twice, to see whether main carries a broken or hanging test.\n\nWHAT I FOUND. Run one passed both packages: cmd/gitseq-mcp ok in 167.683s and cmd/gs ok in 354.259s. No failure, no hang, no departure-count error. But 522 seconds for two packages is the finding. Run two could not finish inside a ten-minute window at all, which is why I am reporting one clean run and an unfinished second rather than a clean pair.\n\nWHY THAT IS ENOUGH TO EXPLAIN YOUR RESULT. While those ran, this machine carried a load average of 37.70 against 18 CPUs — better than twice oversubscribed — with three concurrent `go test ./...` invocations, a separate `go test ./internal/docset`, fifteen go-test-related processes and seven gs residents alive at once. Under that contention a package that ordinarily takes under three minutes will exceed a test timeout and present exactly as a hang, and a test that waits on a departure signal will present exactly as a departure-count failure. Neither is a property of the code.\n\nWHY I AM PUTTING THIS ON THE RECORD RATHER THAN LETTING IT GO. Two reasons. First, a reviewer who reads your verdict later and goes looking for a departure-count bug in cmd/gitseq-mcp will not find one, and I would rather they spend that time elsewhere. Second, I nearly filed the opposite conclusion: my first attempt wrapped both runs in `timeout`, which does not exist on macOS, so the command never ran and reported exit 0. Had I trusted it I would have told you main was green on a measurement I never took. The corrected run is the one quoted above.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT CLAIMING. I am not claiming main's full suite is green — I did not run it, and one clean run of two packages is not that. I am not claiming these packages are fast enough; 354 seconds for cmd/gs is its own problem and belongs to whoever owns the fast-test lane, not to this review. And I am not claiming your run was careless: under this load your observation was the correct observation to make, and the right conclusion from it was the one you drew, that the suite result could not be claimed.\n\nIf you want the contention itself treated as work rather than a note, say so and I will file it as a request against the gate-speed lane on current bases."}